PostgreSQL High-Availability Architecture

1. Cluster Diagram

                       [Load Balancer]
                              |
           -----------------------------------
           |                 |               |
     [Primary Node]    [Replica 1]      [Replica 2]
     (Read/Write)      (Hot Standby)    (Hot Standby)
           |                 |               |
     [Monitoring]      [Monitoring]     [Monitoring]
    (Bash Cron)      (Bash Cron)      (Bash Cron)

Key Components:
- Primary Node: Handles all writes, replicates data to replicas
- Replica Nodes: Hot standbys for read scaling and failover
- Load Balancer: Routes traffic and detects primary failure
- Monitoring: Cron jobs checking status and replication

2. Network Topology
- Private network (10.0.0.0/24)
- Replication via port 5432
- Health checks using pg_isready

3. Failover Mechanism
Failure Detection:
- Cron job runs every 10s on replicas:
  pg_isready -h primary_ip -p 5432 || trigger_failover.sh

Promote Replica:
- trigger_failover.sh promotes replica using:
  pg_ctl promote -D $PGDATA

Recovery Time Objective: 30-60 seconds

4. Monitoring
Script (monitor_node.sh):

#!/bin/bash
if ! pg_isready -q -h localhost; then
  echo "ALERT: PostgreSQL down" | mail admin@example.com
fi

if [ "$(whoami)" = "postgres" ]; then
  lag=$(psql -c "SELECT EXTRACT(SECOND FROM (now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp())) AS lag;" -t)
  if [ "${lag%.*}" -gt 5 ]; then
    echo "ALERT: Replication lag ${lag}s" | mail admin@example.com
  fi
fi

Cron job runs every minute:
* * * * * /usr/local/bin/monitor_node.sh >> /var/log/postgres_monitor.log

5. Design Decisions

Choice: Synchronous Replication
Reason: Ensures no data loss for healthcare records

Choice: Bash + Cron
Reason: Simple to maintain, no external tools needed

Choice: Hot Standbys
Reason: Fast failover capability

Trade-offs:
- Manual load balancer updates after failover
- Risk of split-brain without quorum (mitigated by sync replication)
